 "response": "Gikistu's budget is now **$3,098K**. I worked through the records by hand, so small slips are possible.\n\n**Method**\n1. I ignored every message not from the Finance Office, along with Finance Office notes that don't change the budget (room moves, invoices, audits, reminders).\n2. I resolved the withdrawal chains from the latest record backwards. Where a withdrawal is itself withdrawn, the earlier withdrawal stops counting, so the update it targeted applies again.\n3. I started from the $2,070K set in #0001. I then applied every budget increase and decrease that was not finally withdrawn, in record order.\n\n**Examples of chain resolution**\n- **#0003 (\u2212$108K) is withdrawn.** The chain #0694 \u2192 #0535 \u2192 #0435 \u2192 #0357 \u2192 #0206 \u2192 #0202 \u2192 #0145 \u2192 #0011 \u2192 #0005 ends with #0005 still active.\n- **#0002 (\u2212$139K) applies.** #0843 withdraws #0754, which restores #0019, which withdraws #0008.\n- **#0013 (+$271K) applies.** #0660 withdraws #0274, which restores #0028, which withdraws #0023.\n- **#0036 (\u2212$141K) applies.** The chain from #0964 down ends with #0085 active, which withdraws #0067.\n- **#0525 (+$409K) and #0600 (+$167K) are withdrawn** by #0860 and #1260, which are never themselves withdrawn.\n\n**Running total at a few points**\n| After record | Budget |\n|---|---|\n| #0120 | $796K |\n| #0365 | $2,647K |\n| #0852 | $2,850K |\n| #1278 | $4,223K |\n| #1600 | $3,098K |\n\nANSWER: $3098K",
